Replacing $x$ by $\frac{x}{k}$ gives $y = f\left(\frac{x}{k}\right)$, a horizontal dilation from the $y$-axis that multiplies every $x$-coordinate by $k$. The illustration shows $k = 2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-4.0,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.1) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-3.6:3.2, samples=100, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.05*\x*\x*\x + 0.125*\x*\x - 0.6*\x - 0.6});
\draw[->, red, thick] (-1.5,0.975) -- (-3.0,0.975);
\node[red] at (-2.25,1.5) {$\times 2$};
\node[gray] at (-2.2,-1.6) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.7,-1.6) {$y=f(x/2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Replacing $y$ by $\frac{y}{l}$ instead gives $y = l\,f(x)$, a vertical dilation from the $x$-axis that multiplies every $y$-coordinate by $l$. The illustration shows $l = 2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.1]
\draw[->] (-2.4,0) -- (2.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.8:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.8*\x*\x*\x + 1.0*\x*\x - 2.4*\x - 1.2});
\draw[->, red, thick] (0.667,-1.059) -- (0.667,-2.118);
\node[red, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (0.667,-1.589) {$\times 2$};
\node[gray] at (-1.2,-2.0) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.3,1.6) {$y=2f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

